OOPPTTIIOONNSS
       ----aalllloocc ccoonnttiigguuoouuss|cclliinngg|cclliinngg__bbyy__ttaaggss|nnoorrmmaall|aannyywwhheerree|iinnhheerriitt
              Determines the allocation policy when a command needs to allo-
              cate Physical Extents (PEs) from the VG. Each VG and LV has an
              allocation policy which can be changed with vgchange/lvchange,
              or overriden on the command line.  nnoorrmmaall applies common sense
              rules such as not placing parallel stripes on the same PV.  iinn--
              hheerriitt applies the VG policy to an LV.  ccoonnttiigguuoouuss requires new
              PEs be placed adjacent to existing PEs.  cclliinngg places new PEs on
              the same PV as existing PEs in the same stripe of the LV.  If
              there are sufficient PEs for an allocation, but normal does not
              use them, aannyywwhheerree will use them even if it reduces performance,
              e.g. by placing two stripes on the same PV.  Optional positional
              PV args on the command line can also be used to limit which PVs
              the command will use for allocation.  See llvvmm(8) for more infor-
              mation about allocation.

       --bb|----bbaacckkggrroouunndd
              If the operation requires polling, this option causes the com-
              mand to return before the operation is complete, and polling is
              done in the background.

       --HH|----ccaacchhee
              Specifies the command is handling a cache LV or cache pool.  See
              --type cache and --type cache-pool.  See llvvmmccaacchhee(7) for more
              information about LVM caching.

       ----ccaacchheeddeevviiccee _P_V
              The name of a device to use for a cache.

       ----ccaacchheemmeettaaddaattaaffoorrmmaatt aauuttoo|11|22
              Specifies the cache metadata format used by cache target.

       ----ccaacchheemmooddee wwrriitteetthhrroouugghh|wwrriitteebbaacckk|ppaasssstthhrroouugghh
              Specifies when writes to a cache LV should be considered com-
              plete.  wwrriitteebbaacckk considers a write complete as soon as it is
              stored in the cache pool.  wwrriitteetthhoouugghh considers a write com-
              plete only when it has been stored in both the cache pool and on
              the origin LV.  While writethrough may be slower for writes, it
              is more resilient if something should happen to a device associ-
              ated with the cache pool LV. With ppaasssstthhrroouugghh, all reads are
              served from the origin LV (all reads miss the cache) and all
              writes are forwarded to the origin LV; additionally, write hits
              cause cache block invalidates. See llvvmmccaacchhee(7) for more informa-
              tion.

       ----ccaacchheeppoolliiccyy _S_t_r_i_n_g
              Specifies the cache policy for a cache LV.  See llvvmmccaacchhee(7) for
              more information.

       ----ccaacchheeppooooll _L_V
              The name of a cache pool.

       ----ccaacchheesseettttiinnggss _S_t_r_i_n_g
              Specifies tunable values for a cache LV in "Key = Value" form.
              Repeat this option to specify multiple values.  (The default
              values should usually be adequate.)  The special string value
              ddeeffaauulltt switches settings back to their default kernel values
              and removes them from the list of settings stored in LVM meta-
              data.  See llvvmmccaacchhee(7) for more information.

       ----ccaacchheessiizzee _S_i_z_e[m|UNIT]
              The size of cache to use.

       ----ccaacchheevvooll _L_V
              The name of a cache volume.

       --cc|----cchhuunnkkssiizzee _S_i_z_e[k|UNIT]
              The size of chunks in a snapshot, cache pool or thin pool.  For
              snapshots, the value must be a power of 2 between 4KiB and
              512KiB and the default value is 4.  For a cache pool the value
              must be between 32KiB and 1GiB and the default value is 64.  For
              a thin pool the value must be between 64KiB and 1GiB and the de-
              fault value starts with 64 and scales up to fit the pool meta-
              data size within 128MiB, if the pool metadata size is not speci-
              fied.  The value must be a multiple of 64KiB.  See llvvmmtthhiinn(7)
              and llvvmmccaacchhee(7) for more information.

       ----ccoommmmaannddpprrooffiillee _S_t_r_i_n_g
